        "ContentType": "Description",
        "SortOrder": 0,
        "Content": "\nDispensary Residence, Ballycastle, Co. Mayo...\n\nA Remarkable Five-Bedroom Edwardian Residence Steeped in History\n\nBallycastle Dispensary Residence is a distinguished five-bedroom, two-bathroom Edwardian country home dating from circa 1907, set on approximately 0.85 acres of mature gardens and grounds with attractive sea views towards Downpatrick Head.\n\nOriginally constructed as the residence and surgery of the local dispensary doctor, this unique property retains an abundance of original period features and craftsmanship, complemented by a beautifully refurbished and fully self-contained two-bedroom Coach House.\n\nSet in a peaceful coastal location along the Wild Atlantic Way, the property offers a rare combination of generous accommodation, history, character and lifestyle appeal.\n\nHighlights\n\n• Edwardian residence dating from circa 1907\n• Former residence and surgery of the local dispensary doctor\n• Main residence extending to approx. 223 sq. m. (2,400 sq. ft.), excluding attic space\n• Refurbished, self-contained two-bedroom Coach House\n• Coach House and outbuildings extending to approx. 95 sq. m. (1,023 sq. ft.)\n• Approx. 0.85 acres of mature gardens and grounds\n• Stone outbuildings, workshop space and rear field\n• Attractive sea views towards Downpatrick Head\n• Additional adjoining site of approx. 0.72 acres available by separate negotiation\n\nHistory & Heritage\n\nThe property was built by the Local Government as the residence and surgery of Ballycastle's dispensary doctor, forming part of an important network of dispensary residences established throughout Mayo, Roscommon and Galway.\nThe first dispensary doctor to occupy the residence was Dr. John Crowley, who served Ballycastle from 1907 until 1934. Dr. Crowley later became a notable figure in Irish history, being elected to the First Dáil in 1918 as a Teachta Dála.\nA commemorative monument within the grounds pays tribute to his contribution to both medicine and Irish independence. Several original medical artefacts associated with the property's former use as a doctor's residence and dispensary have also survived and are now held within the Jackie Clarke Museum of Irish History in Ballina. The residence remained in medical use until 1992.\nAdding further to its fascinating history, a drawing of the property by renowned Irish artist Jack B. Yeats was recently discovered in one of his sketchbooks.\n\nCharacter & Craftsmanship\n\nThe main house retains many fine original features including high ceilings, original fireplaces, decorative plasterwork, pitch pine doors and architraves, panelled timber doors, sash windows with shutters and generous room proportions.\nConstructed using stone masonry, Scandinavian pine roof timbers and red engineering brick detailing, the residence reflects the quality of workmanship associated with the Edwardian period. The property is eligible for the SEAI grants..\n\nAccommodation\n\nFloor plans are available in the photos.\n\nMain Residence\n\nGround Floor\n• Entrance porch and original hallway \n• Two principal reception rooms \n• Kitchen with serving hatch to front reception room \n• Utility / back kitchen \n• Bathroom \n• Former dispensary surgery, offering excellent potential as a home office, studio or creative workspace \n• Former waiting room with adjoining bathroom \n• Elegant original staircase leading from the entrance hall to the split-level and first-floor accommodation \n\nSplit-Level / Half First Floor\n\n• Bedroom \n• Bathroom \n• Hot press \n\nFirst Floor\n\n• Four generous double bedrooms \n• Spacious landing \n\nCoach House\n\nThe beautifully refurbished Coach House is a particularly attractive addition to the property. Fully self-contained, it provides excellent flexibility for guest accommodation, extended family living, holiday letting or long-term rental use, subject to the necessary permissions.\n\nGround Floor\n\n• Open-plan living / kitchen area with vaulted ceiling and skylight \n• Double bedroom \n• Bathroom \n• Walk-in wardrobe / storage area \n\nFirst Floor\n\n• Large bedroom \n• Raised cabin sleeping berth \n• En-suite bathroom \n• Walk-in wardrobe \n\nGrounds & Setting\n\nThe mature grounds include established trees, gardens, stone outbuildings and a rear field. Two substantial stone sheds, formerly used as stables and storage, provide excellent workshop and storage space with potential for a variety of uses.\nAn additional adjoining site of approximately 0.72 acres may be available by separate negotiation and is connected to mains water and the public sewer network.\n\nLocation & Lifestyle\n\nThe property is approximately 700m from Ballycastle village, with local shops, primary school, café and pubs. Ballycastle Beach is less than 3 km away, with Lacken Strand, Ross Strand and the wider North Mayo coastline all within easy reach.\nBallinglen Arts Foundation is approximately 1 km away, while Downpatrick Head and the world-famous Céide Fields are also nearby. The developing Céide Coastal Walk will further enhance the area's appeal for walking, tourism and outdoor recreation.\n\nApproximate distances:\n• 700m Ballycastle village\n• 1 km Ballinglen Arts Foundation\n• Less than 3 km Ballycastle Beach\n• 10 km Lacken Strand\n• 12 km Ross Strand / Killala\n• 26 km Ballina\n• 40 km Enniscrone Beach\n\nA Property of Exceptional Individuality..\n\nBallycastle Dispensary Residence is a property of genuine historical and architectural significance. Its period character, fascinating medical heritage, artistic connection, mature grounds and coastal setting combine to create a truly special home.\nWhether suited to family living, multi-generational use, guest accommodation or a variety of lifestyle and creative purposes, this is a rare opportunity to acquire an important piece of Ballycastle's history in one of North Mayo's most beautiful coastal settings."
